Q: Java Trying to create a class to print ArrayList to File A: <p>pw must be a class level variable, currently the scope is inside method and it can not be accessed outside the method. </p>

<pre><code>import java.util.*;
import java.io.*;
    PrintWriter pw = null; // You need to declare it here
public class PrintToFile{
    public void PrintToFile()throws Exception{
      File f = new File ("Output.txt");
      FileWriter fw = new FileWriter(f, true);
      pw = new PrintWriter(fw);
   }

    public void printExp(ArrayList&lt;Expense&gt; expList){
        for(int i = 0; i &lt; expList.size(); i++){
              if(pw != null){ // need to check for null, as  it might be null
                  pw.println("---------------------------------------");//line 13
                  pw.println(expList.get(i));
              }

      }
        pw.close();
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Variables declared as method parameters can be accessed from within the method body. Class-level variables are accessible from anywhere in the class.</p>

<p>If a method declares a local variable that has the same name as a class-level variable, the former will 'shadow' the latter. To access the class-level variable from inside the method body, use the this keyword.</p>

<p>For more details on variable scope, you can see <a href="http://www.java-made-easy.com/variable-scope.html" rel="nofollow">this</a></p>

Q: Output to file from arraylist not working - java A: <p>The <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/20272243/642706">Answer by rzymek</a> is correct.</p>
<p>Here is alternative code using modern Java features such as Streams, method references, NIO.2 (<code>Paths</code> &amp; <code>Files</code>, etc.), <code>IO</code> for <code>System.out</code>.</p>
<p>👉🏽 The key idea is to pass a list of strings to <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html#write(java.nio.file.Path,java.lang.Iterable,java.nio.charset.Charset,java.nio.file.OpenOption...)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Files.write</code></a>. That one method handles opening and closing the file, buffered writing, etc.</p>
<p>So the first step is to build a <code>List</code> of <code>String</code> objects to serve as lines for our text file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>List &lt; Integer &gt; energyList = … ;
List &lt; String &gt; lines = energyList.stream().map( Integer :: toString ).toList() ;
</code></pre>
<p>Write that list of lines to a file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Path filePath = Path.of( &quot;/path/to/example.txt&quot; );
try
{
    Files.write( filePath , lines ); // Defaults character encoding to UTF-8.
    IO.println( &quot;Successfully written to: &quot; + filePath.toAbsolutePath() );
}
catch ( IOException e )
{
    System.err.println( &quot;Error writing to file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
    e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
